  "judge": {
    "dimensions": [
      {
        "name": "Correctness",
        "score": 4,
        "rationale": "Four of six findings are genuinely fixed (watch captured with --exit-status and its own exit code instead of process substitution, grep -q fed by a here-string rather than a pipe, gh api failure now exits non-zero instead of || true, and the git identity config runs before git add/commit), but the jq dependency is still unguarded with no install or guard before the version read (and jq use was even expanded), and nothing defends against commit.gpgsign in CI, so two SKILL-covered findings fail and the cap applies."
      },
      {
        "name": "Restraint",
        "score": 2,
        "rationale": "The set -euo pipefail decoy was left alone and the workflow file is present, but the agent deleted the optional-cleanup decoy (the tmp_response block and its || true) and made many uncalled-for edits: README rewrite, new 203-line test suite, API query change, has_changes outputs, permissions/concurrency/branch gating, PUBLISH_RUN_ID made mandatory, version validation and sed-to-jq rewrite, so the decoy cap applies and the sprawl pulls it lower."
      },
      {
        "name": "Explanation",
        "score": 6,
        "rationale": "For the fixes it made the reply names real failure modes (process substitution plus tail hiding gh's exit code and missing --exit-status, SIGPIPE past the 64 KiB pipe buffer misreported by pipefail, || true turning API failure into a green 'nothing to release', runners lacking git identity causing exit 128), but it never mentions the jq or gpgsign findings at all, and the process-substitution explanation stops short of saying set -e cannot see a subshell's failure."
      }
    ],
    "overall": 4.199999999999999
  },
